# {{ .Vars.research_dir }}/ — what is true about the world outside this repository

One question per file, answered from sources, with a date after which nobody may
trust the answer without looking again.

This is not the place for how *this* system works — that is
`{{ .Vars.group }}/reference/`. It is the place for the things a session spends its
first twenty minutes rediscovering: which API version actually works, what the
vendor's rate limit really is, why the obvious library was rejected.

## Frontmatter

```yaml
---
id: res-stripe-rate-limits           # stable; other documents link by it
title: Stripe's real rate limit on the Payment Intents API
status: current
question: How many Payment Intents requests can we make per second before 429s, and what does Stripe do at the limit?
sources:                             # the pages you actually opened
  - https://docs.stripe.com/rate-limits
  - https://github.com/stripe/stripe-node/issues/1234
confidence: high                     # high | medium | low
expires: 2026-11-05                  # after this date, re-read before trusting
updated: 2026-08-07
---
```

`confidence:` means something specific:

| Value | Means |
|---|---|
| `high` | Read from the vendor's own documentation, or observed directly against the real thing, and the sources agree. |
| `medium` | One source, or several that agree but none authoritative. |
| `low` | Inferred, or the sources disagree. A `low` finding is a lead, not an answer — say so wherever you use it. |

## Body

Two sections carry weight. The rest is context.

```markdown
## Finding

The answer, in the fewest words that are still precise. Numbers, versions and
error codes, not impressions.

## What would change this

- Stripe publishes a different number on the rate-limits page.
- We see a 429 below the stated rate in production.
- The Payment Intents API reaches a new major version.
```

`## What would change this` is required, and it is the section that does the work. A
conclusion nothing could overturn is an opinion wearing a citation, and this directory
is read as though everything in it had been checked.

## Why these expire, when the rest of the record does not

Everywhere else, ilk measures staleness by **coupling**: a document goes stale when
the code it describes changes, so a stable subsystem never expires and a churning one
expires immediately. The project's own pace sets the pace of review.

That signal does not exist here. A vendor halving a rate limit, a library shipping a
major version, a deprecation reaching its date — none of them touch a file in this
repository, so no amount of watching it will ever notice. A date the author chose is
the only handle there is, and `expires:` is that date.

Choose it from how fast the source moves, not from habit:

| Subject | A reasonable first guess |
|---|---|
| Vendor pricing, quotas, rate limits | 90 days |
| A library's API within a pinned major version | 180 days |
| Anything in beta or preview | 30 days |
| An announced deprecation | the announced date |

```
ilk autoresearch due          # what expires within {{ .Vars.warn_before_days }} days
ilk autoresearch due 60       # or any window
```

A finding past its date fails `ilk check`. Renewing that date is a claim that you
opened a source again — the one habit a timer must not train is bumping a number to
get a build green.

## When a finding turns out to be wrong

Correct it in place and say what was believed, so the next reader can calibrate how
much this directory is worth. A finding that was quietly rewritten looks exactly like
one that was right all along, and the difference is the whole value of the record.
